\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Keep the square fixed while removing the mesh}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{A [point-sampled field](fdip-00HU) averages many lattice values
against one smooth function. As the mesh tends to zero, individual
point variances grow while such averages can converge. The remaining
boundary still changes their covariance. How can the limiting random
distribution retain the Dirichlet boundary and the same whole-space
quartic ordering? The fixed-region construction is given by
\citet{Theorem IV.13}{guerra1975euclideanII}.}
